Jr has had difficulty moving since  suffering a stroke last December he  can't walk up the stairs in his home so  when flood waters rose on Sunday he and  his family had to evacuate  they said it was a super typhoon we  thought we'd get flooded for sure so we  left for safer grounds true enough the  flood came  super typhoon noru dumped torrential  rains as it crossed the Philippines  largest island Luzon  fortunately for people in Manila the  downpour stopped just as Rivers began to  overflow  by Monday morning those who fled to  safety were back cleaning up their homes  while government workers cleared their  streets  there were fears the Philippine Capital  would suffer catastrophic flooding  reminiscent of that of typhoon kitsana  back in 2009 but it was largely spared  the local environment officer of this  Manila suburb says the dredging of  waterways also made a difference  we were dredging every day so that gave  the river the capacity to hold more rain  president Ferdinand Marcus Jr also  praised the preparedness of the  government and of the people  I think uh though it's clear  from this uh  from what we did this last two days is  that very very important is preparation  get people out of the areas of danger  put all of your assets that you're going  to use  despite those preparations noru was  destructive several rescue workers  became the first known victims of the  storm an entire villages in the  countryside remain flooded  many fear the full extent of the damage  is only just starting to become clear  Al Jazeera Manila
 

